\details{
This is the replacement for \code{\link[=pr_calc_gait_cycles]{pr_calc_gait_cycles()}} on mats that stay
loaded. That function pairs a threshold crossing up with the next
crossing down; a saddle mat never crosses back down, so it returns a
single "cycle" spanning the whole recording no matter how
\code{force_threshold} is set. The problem is the segmentation model, not its
tuning, so nothing about \code{pr_calc_gait_cycles()} is changed here.

The pipeline, in order:
\enumerate{
\item \strong{Signal.} \code{signal} selects the frame-level series exactly as in
\code{\link[=pr_calc_spectrum]{pr_calc_spectrum()}}. \code{"total"} and \code{"mean"} differ by the constant
\code{n_sensors}, so they give identical segmentation; only \code{peak_total}
changes scale.
\item \strong{Low pass.} An \code{lp_window}-sample centred moving average. Frames at
the two ends, where the window overhangs the recording, keep their raw
value rather than becoming \code{NA}, so the first and last strides are
still detectable.
\item \strong{High pass.} Subtract a running median over \code{hp_window_s} seconds.
The median steps over the stride peaks instead of being pulled up by
them, so the residual keeps its amplitude while the baseline drift —
the rider settling, the horse changing gait — is removed.
\item \strong{Peaks.} Strict local maxima of the residual at or above
\code{stats::quantile(residual[residual > 0], height_quantile)}, thinned
tallest-first so no two peaks sit closer than
\code{round(min_period_s * fs)} samples.
\item \strong{Cycles.} Every consecutive pair of peaks, keeping those whose
duration lies in \verb{[min_period_s, max_period_s]}.
}

\code{cycle} numbers the peak pairs \strong{before} the duration filter, so a gap
in the sequence marks an interval that was rejected as too short or too
long — information a renumbered column would hide.

\code{peak_total} is the band-passed amplitude at the cycle's opening peak, in
the device's pressure unit. It is an excursion above the local baseline,
not a raw frame total: on the reference recording the frame totals run
383-434 kPa while \code{peak_total} runs 2.5-13.8 kPa.
}
\section{Agreement with the source study}{

On the 869-frame reference recording \code{ID052_K_K_KG00_MS}, the defaults
reproduce the study's own segmentation exactly: 14 cycles, identical
\code{cycle}, \code{start_idx}, \code{end_idx}, \code{duration_s}, \code{peak_total} and
\code{stride_freq} to machine precision. Rerun over the study's whole cohort,
all 323 of its 50 Hz recordings match cycle for cycle — 137,761 cycles,
no differences.

The remaining 108 recordings were sampled at 10 Hz, and there the two
disagree. The study hard-coded its running-median width at 251 samples
and its minimum peak separation at 15 samples, which are 5 s and 0.3 s
only at 50 Hz; the parameters here are in seconds and scale with the
trial's own sampling rate. Passing \code{hp_window_s = 25.1} and
\code{min_period_s = 1.5} reproduces the study's fixed sample counts on a
10 Hz recording.

That difference shows up in the published cohort means. All 34,095 of
the study's slow-gait (\code{MG}) cycles come from 50 Hz recordings, and this
function returns their published mean duration of 0.719 s exactly. The
\code{MH} and \code{MS} means, 0.748 s and 0.773 s, mix in 10 Hz recordings; on
the 50 Hz half alone both this function and the study give 0.650 s and
0.726 s.
}

